  "prompt_text": "This figure is a schematic cross-section of soil showing how organisms and physical structures shape the profile from the surface down through the O, A, B, and C horizons. A tree dominates the left side, with a wide, branching root system concentrated in the organic-rich upper layers and extending into deeper soil. The image emphasizes that roots are a major driver of structure in the A horizon, where much of the biological activity seems to occur.\n\nOn the right, smaller grass-like plants and an earthworm highlight other important biological agents near the surface. The worm appears to represent bioturbation and mixing of soil material, while plant roots contribute channels and aggregation. A narrow vertical white feature in the center looks like a crack or macropore, suggesting a pathway for water, air, and root penetration through the profile.\n\nAn inset at the lower right shows soil aggregates or particles at about centimeter scale. Larger clods are labeled differently from smaller ones, indicating a comparison between aggregate sizes or structural units. Overall, the figure conveys that soil architecture emerges from interactions among roots, soil fauna, pore spaces, and mineral material, with the strongest visible biological influence concentrated near the topsoil.",
